bpa(){return A.a1U(!0,"Games",A.b([A.zh(new A.aTk(),"https://github.com/adario/rasterizer_flutter/blob/main/flame_rasterizer/example/lib/stories/games/svg_shooter","    A vertical shooter drawn with SVGs. The player's spaceship is an SVG in\n    colour, and the enemies and the missiles are wireframe SVGs.\n    RasterizerSvgComponent draws them with the renderer that the Renderer knob\n    picks; with Flame SVG, flame_svg's SvgComponent does.\n\n    Move the spaceship by dragging anywhere, or with the arrow keys or WASD.\n    Fire while dragging, or while holding the space bar. Once the game is over,\n    tap to start a new one.\n\n    Waves of enemies fall from the top: two kinds of spaceships, rockets and\n    swinging robots, in random colours. Each enemy that hits the spaceship\n    takes some of its health, which comes back after a while without a hit.\n    The game is over when the health runs out.\n\n    Hundreds of SVGs are on screen at once, each with an SvgHitbox in the shape\n    of its paths. The stars are sprites, and the explosions are particles. The\n    other knobs zoom the camera and change the game's speed; with Flame SVG,\n    the cache knobs set flame_svg's cache. Only a new renderer or cache makes\n    a new game. A new renderer's game goes on with the same score, spaceship,\n    enemies and missiles, from where they were. The health, the score and the number\n    of components are at the bottom right. The bar under the game shows the\n    renderer, the frame times and the renderer's own numbers.\n\n    Based on Flame's Rogue Shooter example game.\n  ","Svg Shooter"),A.zh(new A.aTl(),"https://github.com/adario/rasterizer_flutter/blob/main/flame_rasterizer/example/lib/stories/games/tube_shooter","    A tube shooter in vector lines. Everything in the game, text included, is\n    a Flutter Path. RasterizerPathComponent draws them with the renderer that\n    the Renderer knob picks; with Flame SVG, Flame's PathComponent does.\n\n    Move the ship round the rim of the tube with the arrow keys, A and D, by\n    dragging left and right, or by a lane per scroll of the mouse wheel. On\n    Android and iOS, a finger that circles round the tube's centre moves it\n    instead. On a Steam Deck, a trackpad set to Steam Input's Scroll Wheel\n    mode turns it round as a finger circles. Fire down its lane with the\n    space bar, or while dragging. Enter, or a double tap, destroys all the\n    enemies in the tube, one time per round. Tap or press a key to start a\n    round, and to continue after losing a ship.\n\n    Five kinds of enemies climb the tube. When they're all gone, the ship\n    drops down its lane, and any spike left in that lane destroys it. Then it\n    flies down Tube Runner's endless tube, by that game's rules, while the\n    round's enemies come again in the same order. After them, it flies out\n    onto the rim of the next of ten tubes. Each round is 5% faster than the\n    last, and a game that starts at a later level is as fast as if it had\n    started at the first. After Game Over, a new game starts at the round\n    reached, as fast.\n\n    The game makes some paths again every frame, such as the tube's during the\n    zoom. It shares others among many components, such as the enemies'\n    shapes. The Glow knob makes the lines glow: the mesh renderer draws the\n    glow with them, and the other renderers draw it with a shader over the\n    world. The Level knob shows the round's level, and starts a new game at\n    the level it picks. The other knobs turn the sounds on and off, zoom the\n    camera and change the game's speed. A new renderer's game continues from\n    where the old one was, with the same round, ship, enemies and shots. If\n    the ship was flying out of a run onto the next rim, that round starts\n    again from its zoom. The bar under the game shows the renderer, the frame\n    times, the renderer's own numbers and the paths made each second.\n\n    Inspired by Atari's Tempest (1981).\n  ","Tube Shooter"),A.zh(new A.aTm(),"https://github.com/adario/rasterizer_flutter/blob/main/flame_rasterizer/example/lib/stories/games/revolute_pinball","    A pinball with the bodies of Revolute Joint Svgs. A key or a tap starts\n    the game and drops 10 bodies over a wheel. When each of them is in a\n    bucket, has drained, or is at rest, the wheel turns the other way, amber\n    until it reaches full speed, and the next 10 drop. Left, A or Left Shift\n    flips the left flipper, and Right, D or Right Shift the right one. So\n    does a touch in the left or right half of the bottom third. On a Steam\n    Deck, Steam Input can bind the triggers to the Shift keys. A body scores\n    one time, when it comes to rest in a bucket: the bigger the body and the\n    fuller the bucket, the more it scores. Each drained body takes energy by\n    its area. When the energy runs out, the game is over, and the table keeps\n    moving with no flippers, drops or scores. A full bucket gives back the\n    energy its bodies would take if drained, empties, and kicks the bodies\n    in the other bucket up and out. The Drops knob picks the bodies of the\n    next drops: mixed, only balls or only SVGs. The Sound knob turns the\n    sounds on and off.\n  ","Revolute Pinball")],t.Jw))},
